\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Patch-wise Transformers (e.g., PatchTST) markedly improve long-horizon time-series forecasting via local-context patches and longer effective memory}}{2}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{while TimesFM demonstrates zero-/few-shot forecasting from large-scale pretraining, suggesting that data scale and pretraining are key drivers of transformer gains}}{2}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In finance, domain LLMs (BloombergGPT, FinGPT) and multimodal pipelines point to text+price integration as a promising route}}{2}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{our negative result for TFT on a small, daily panel aligns with this view---that Transformers often need more data and richer modalities to consistently outperform simpler sequence models.}}{2}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The coefficient of determination $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ is computed as:}}{2}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}= 1 - \frac {\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _i {(y_i - \hat {y}_i)}^2}{\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _i {(y_i - \bar {y})}^2}}}{2}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{where $y_i$ are actual returns, $\hat {y}_i$ are predictions, and $\bar {y}$ is the mean return. Crucially, $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ can be negative when the model performs worse than simply predicting the mean return ($\bar {y} = 0$ for our zero-return baseline). Negative $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ values indicate that the model's predictions have higher mean squared error than the naive baseline of always predicting zero return, which is common in financial time series due to their inherently low signal-to-noise ratio.}}{3}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{While $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ measures explained variance, \ensuremath {\mathrm {RMSE}}{} quantifies absolute prediction errors in interpretable units (log-return scale), and \ensuremath {\mathrm {DA}}{} captures directional prediction accuracy—crucial for trading applications where sign matters more than magnitude. These complementary metrics provide a complete performance picture: $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ reveals whether models beat naive baselines, \ensuremath {\mathrm {RMSE}}{} measures prediction precision, and \ensuremath {\mathrm {DA}}{} evaluates practical trading signal quality.}}{3}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{\emph {Capacity \& regularization.} Because our daily dataset is small, we used a smaller TFT (fewer attention heads and layers, more dropout) to prevent overfitting. To ensure fairness, we tested larger TFT configurations with different regularization settings (details in Appendix B.2). While larger TFTs sometimes performed better on validation data, they didn't show consistent improvements on test data for the 21-day horizon. Therefore, we stick with the smaller configuration for our main results.}}{3}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{We constrained TFT capacity (fewer layers/heads) to reduce overfitting on a small daily dataset. Results should therefore be read conditional on this conservative setting. Higher-capacity or pretrained Transformers may perform better under richer data or modalities, which we leave for future work.}}{3}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Blocks represent encoders for static features, known future covariates, and observed history; the fused representation feeds multi-horizon output heads for $h\in \{1,5,21\}$.}}{4}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{We use a compact configuration (1 layer, 4 heads) to prevent overfitting on the daily panel.}}{4}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{All $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ values are near zero or negative, which is both expected and meaningful in financial prediction. Negative $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ indicates that models perform worse than the naive zero-return baseline—a common outcome in equity prediction due to the inherently low signal-to-noise ratio and market efficiency. This does not imply model failure; rather, it reflects the fundamental difficulty of predicting stock returns where random walk behavior dominates systematic patterns.}}{4}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The negative $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ values highlight why \ensuremath {\mathrm {RMSE}}{} and \ensuremath {\mathrm {DA}}{} provide crucial complementary insights. While $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ measures variance explained relative to a baseline, \ensuremath {\mathrm {RMSE}}{} quantifies absolute prediction accuracy in interpretable units, and \ensuremath {\mathrm {DA}}{} captures the economically relevant question of directional correctness. For trading applications, correctly predicting direction (even with poor variance explanation) can be more valuable than minimizing squared errors while missing trends.}}{4}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Bars show \ensuremath {\mathrm {DA}}{} (higher is better) for $h\in \{1,5,21\}$, averaged over three random seeds.}}{4}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{LSTM/GRU improve from roughly 51–52\% at $h{=}1$ to about 55\% at longer horizons, while tabular baselines (Ridge, Random Forest, XGBoost) change little or degrade slightly relative to RNNs; TFT is competitive but does not lead.}}{4}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{This pattern suggests temporal sequence models benefit more from longer context when predicting multi-week moves, whereas purely tabular models capture less horizon-dependent signal.}}{4}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{LSTM is best at $h{=}21$ (lowest RMSE 0.05084; highest DA 55.0\%), while Ridge wins $h{=}5$ (RMSE 0.03651), and RF tops DA at $h{=}1$ (51.9\%). TFT is competitive but does not dominate on this daily panel.}}{5}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Each cell is the $h{=}21$ \ensuremath {\mathrm {RMSE}}{} computed on the test split restricted to the indicated period; colors are normalized within each panel.}}{5}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Errors rise for all models in 2022 (high volatility) and fall in 2023–24, with the best model switching from GRU (bear) to LSTM (rally), underscoring regime sensitivity and the value of regime-aware model selection.}}{5}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{GRU is best on RMSE in the 2022 bear; LSTM best in the 2023--24 rally with top DA tied.}}{6}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The universally negative or near-zero $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ values reflect fundamental characteristics of equity prediction rather than model inadequacy. In efficient markets, returns approximate random walks with minimal predictable components, making negative $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ expected rather than failure. Random Forest shows the most negative values (-0.248 at $h=21$), suggesting overfitting, while LSTM achieves values near zero (-0.002), indicating better calibration.}}{6}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The disconnect between $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ and other metrics highlights why multi-metric evaluation is essential. Models can have poor variance explanation yet provide economically valuable directional signals: LSTM achieves 55\% directional accuracy at $h=21$ despite $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}\approx 0$, detecting weak but persistent directional biases.}}{6}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The negative $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ values across models ($\approx -0.25$ for Random Forest to $\approx -0.002$ for LSTM) reflect market efficiency rather than model failure—even our best models struggle to beat the zero-return baseline. However, the relative differences are meaningful: LSTM's near-zero values suggest better calibration, while Random Forest's highly negative values indicate overfitting to noise.}}{6}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{This highlights why \ensuremath {\mathrm {RMSE}}{} and \ensuremath {\mathrm {DA}}{} provide crucial complementary information. While $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}$ measures explained variance, trading applications care more about prediction errors and directional correctness. LSTM achieves both lowest \ensuremath {\mathrm {RMSE}}{} and highest \ensuremath {\mathrm {DA}}{} at $h=21$ despite $\ensuremath {\mathrm {R}^2}\approx 0$, demonstrating economically valuable signals despite minimal variance explanation.}}{6}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{\textbf {Economic significance (no backtesting here).} We focus on statistical accuracy (RMSE, $R^2$, DA) but don't test whether our predictions would actually make money. In practice, profitability depends on trading costs, slippage, how often you trade, and position sizing. A complete evaluation would combine predictions with a trading strategy, measure profit and loss, Sharpe ratio, maximum drawdown, and test sensitivity to transaction costs. We leave this for future work and note that statistical improvements may not survive real trading costs, especially for daily trading.}}{7}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{\textbf {Compute \& efficiency.} Model complexity varies significantly across architectures. Ridge regression is the most efficient (minimal parameters, seconds to train). Random Forest uses 100 trees with max depth 5 ($\sim $10k effective parameters). XGBoost employs max depth 3, learning rate 0.1, 100 boosting rounds. LSTM/GRU models use 1 layer with 64 hidden units ($\sim $25k parameters), training with Adam at learning rate $1\times 10^{-3}$ for up to 10 epochs with early stopping. TFT is the most complex: 4 attention heads, 1 transformer layer, hidden dimensionality 16, dropout 0.3, totaling $\approx $50k parameters, trained with learning rate $3\times 10^{-4}$. Training time scales accordingly: tabular models (Ridge/XGBoost) train in minutes, sequence models (LSTM/GRU) require hours, and TFT incurs the highest computational cost. Given the small daily panel and batch size of 256, simpler models deliver competitive accuracy at substantially lower computational cost, favoring rapid retraining and deployment scenarios.}}{7}{}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{\textbf {Limitations and threats to validity.} Our study is limited to large U.S. stocks (Dow 30) at daily frequency; results may not apply to small companies, international markets, or higher-frequency data where market structure and liquidity are different. We kept the scope narrow to focus on methodological differences, but this limits how broadly our findings apply. We analyze bear (2022) versus bull (2023-2024) markets but don't separately examine extreme events like market crashes. Models trained on historical data often fail during such extremes; future work should test performance during the most volatile periods. Our hyperparameter tuning was limited, which may favor simpler models. Comparing single-horizon models (tabular/RNN) to multi-horizon TFT raises fairness questions, though we tried to address this where possible. Future research should include richer data types (sentiment, macroeconomic data), different loss functions, and interpretability analysis to understand what drives performance in different market conditions.}}{7}{}%
